064e\n\u0631\n\u064e\n\u0627 \u064e\u064a \u0643\n\u064e\n\u062a\u0650 \u062d\u064e\n\u064e \u0652\n\u0631\n\u064e\n\u0650\u0628\n\u064a\n\u064e\n\u0644\u064e\u0650\u064e \u0627\u0639\n\u064e\n\u0652\u0644\u0627 \u0628\n\u064e\u0651\u064e\n\u0631\n\u064e\n\u0627 \u064e\u064a \u064a\n\u064e\n\u0645\u0650 \u0622 \u0645\n\u064e\u0651\u064e\n\u0647\n\u064f\n\u064e\u0651\u064e\u0644\u0644\u0627 \u0645\n\u064e\u0651\u064e\n\u0647\n\u064f\n\u064e\u0651\u064e\u0644\u0644\u0627", "id": "716b667e-73d4-4fb7-a002-4079a7b24521__p8__c14"}, {"doc_id": "d267cf8d-f979-4513-ae3c-fa641358adae", "filename": "research_report_ok (1).pdf", "page": 1, "chunk_index": 0, "uploaded_at": "2026-03-15T20:05:31.163537+00:00", "text": "AI RESEARCH REPORT\nAI Research Report\nMarch 14, 2026\nBuilt by Abdullah Shafique | github.com/aadi-abdullah Page 1 | 2026-03-14", "id": "d267cf8d-f979-4513-ae3c-fa641358adae__p1__c0"}, {"doc_id": "d267cf8d-f979-4513-ae3c-fa641358adae", "filename": "research_report_ok (1).pdf", "page": 2, "chunk_index": 1, "uploaded_at": "2026-03-15T20:05:31.163537+00:00", "text": "AI RESEARCH REPORT\nAI Research Report\nSummary\nIntroduction to OK\nThe term \"OK\" is an English word that originated in American English, denoting approval, acceptance,\nagreement, assent, acknowledgment, or indifference. It is frequently used as a loanword in other languages\nand is considered the most frequently spoken or written word on the planet.\nOrigin of OK\nThe origin of \"OK\" is disputed, but most modern reference works suggest that it originated in Boston in the\nlate 1830s as part of a fad of abbreviating misspellings. According to linguist Allen Walker Read, \"OK\" is an\ninitialism of \"oll korrect,\" a misspelling of \"all correct.\" This theory was first described by Read in the 1960s.\nMeaning and Usage", "id": "d267cf8d-f979-4513-ae3c-fa641358adae__p2__c1"}, {"doc_id": "d267cf8d-f979-4513-ae3c-fa641358adae", "filename": "research_report_ok (1).pdf", "page": 2, "chunk_index": 2, "uploaded_at": "2026-03-15T20:05:31.163537+00:00", "text": "Read, \"OK\" is an\ninitialism of \"oll korrect,\" a misspelling of \"all correct.\" This theory was first described by Read in the 1960s.\nMeaning and Usage\nAs an adjective, \"OK\" means \"adequate\" or \"acceptable,\" and can also imply \"mediocre\" when used in\ncontrast with \"good.\" As an adverb, it can denote compliance or agreement. As an interjection, it can\nexpress assent or acknowledgement. \"OK\" can also be used as a noun to denote agreement or permission.\nCultural Variations\nIn Mainland China, the native term \"h?o\" (meaning \"good\") is more commonly used instead of \"OK.\"\nHowever, \"OK\" is sometimes used when communicating with foreigners, and is often modified to \"OK le\" to\nfit Mandarin grammar. In Taiwan, \"OK\" is frequently used in various sentences, particularly among younger\ngenerations.", "id": "d267cf8d-f979-4513-ae3c-fa641358adae__p2__c2"}, {"doc_id": "d267cf8d-f979-4513-ae3c-fa641358adae", "filename": "research_report_ok (1).pdf", "page": 2, "chunk_index": 3, "uploaded_at": "2026-03-15T20:05:31.163537+00:00", "text": "s often modified to \"OK le\" to\nfit Mandarin grammar. In Taiwan, \"OK\" is frequently used in various sentences, particularly among younger\ngenerations.\nHistorical Context\nThe term \"OK\" gained popularity in the 1840s, particularly during the presidential campaign of Martin Van\nBuren, whose supporters organized a group called the \"O.K. Club.\" The opposing Whig Party also used\n\"OK\" to denigrate Van Buren's political mentor, Andrew Jackson.\nDefinitions and Synonyms\n\"OK\" can be defined as an adjective, adverb, or noun, and is synonymous with terms such as \"all right,\"\n\"fine,\" and \"satisfactory.\" It is often used to ask if something is acceptable or to check on someone's\nwell-being.\nUnique Characteristics", "id": "d267cf8d-f979-4513-ae3c-fa641358adae__p2__c3"}, {"doc_id": "d267cf8d-f979-4513-ae3c-fa641358adae", "filename": "research_report_ok (1).pdf", "page": 2, "chunk_index": 4, "uploaded_at": "2026-03-15T20:05:31.163537+00:00", "text": "ll right,\"\n\"fine,\" and \"satisfactory.\" It is often used to ask if something is acceptable or to check on someone's\nwell-being.\nUnique Characteristics\n\"OK\" is the only known example of a 19th-century slang trend of abbreviating deliberate misspellings that is\nstill in use today. Its versatility and widespread use have made it a ubiquitous term in modern language.\nBuilt by Abdullah Shafique | github.com/aadi-abdullah Page 2 | 2026-03-14", "id": "d267cf8d-f979-4513-ae3c-fa641358adae__p2__c4"}, {"doc_id": "d267cf8d-f979-4513-ae3c-fa641358adae", "filename": "research_report_ok (1).pdf", "page": 3, "chunk_index": 5, "uploaded_at": "2026-03-15T20:05:31.163537+00:00", "text": "AI RESEARCH REPORT\nSources\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OK\nhttps://www.oed.com/dictionary/ok_adj\nhttps://dictionary.cambridge.org/us/dictionary/english/ok\nhttps://www.history.com/this-day-in-history/march-23/ok-enters-national-vernacular\nhttps://www.vocabulary.com/dictionary/ok\nFinal Insight\nAI agents are transforming automation and research workflows.\nBuilt by Abdullah Shafique | github.com/aadi-abdullah Page 3 | 2026-03-14", "id": "d267cf8d-f979-4513-ae3c-fa641358adae__p3__c5"}]